   $q$A bike rental shop offers two plans. Plan A costs 30 dollars plus 2 dollars per hour. Plan B costs 12 dollars plus 5 dollars per hour. Hours are charged at the same rate for any fraction of an hour, so $h$ can be any positive number. For which numbers of hours $h$ is Plan A strictly cheaper than Plan B (not equal in cost)?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$h < 6$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"forgot-to-flip-inequality"},{"text":"$h > \\frac{18}{7}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"added-variable-terms-instead-of-subtracting"},{"text":"$h > 14$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"added-instead-of-subtracted"},{"text":"$h > 6$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$h > 6$$q$, $q$Plan A is cheaper when $30 + 2h < 12 + 5h$. Subtract $2h$ and $12$ from both sides: $18 < 3h$. Divide by $3$: $6 < h$, which is $h > 6$. Check with $h = 7$: Plan A costs 44 dollars and Plan B costs 47 dollars.$q$, 'draft', null)
